  Rivers are among the oldest and most important features of the natural world, shaping landscapes, sustaining ecosystems, and
  supporting human civilization for thousands of years. Born from rainfall, snowmelt, or springs high in mountains and highlands,
  they gather water into streams and tributaries that gradually merge into larger channels as they descend toward the sea. Along
  the way, flowing water carves valleys, transports vast quantities of sediment, and deposits rich soils on floodplains and
  deltas, which is why so many of the world's earliest agricultural societies arose along the banks of rivers such as the Nile,
  the Tigris and Euphrates, the Yangtze, and the Ganges. Rivers also serve as natural highways, enabling trade, migration, and
  communication long before roads and railways existed. Their waters provide drinking water, irrigation, and power for mills and,
  later, hydroelectric dams, while their wetlands and riparian forests host fish, birds, amphibians, and countless invertebrates.
  At the same time, rivers are dynamic and sometimes dangerous: they meander, shift course, flood their surroundings, and reshape
  the land in response to seasonal rains and long-term climate patterns. Human activity has altered many of them through dams,
  levees, pollution, and water extraction, which has raised concerns about habitat loss, declining fish populations, and the 
  health of communities that depend on them. Today, protecting rivers means balancing economic needs with ecological health, and
  it requires cooperation across borders, since many of the world's great rivers flow through several countries before reaching
  the ocean.

